  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 400 AM EDT FRI SEP 11 2026\n\nModel suites are in reasonable agreement for much of the long term \nperiod. The period begins Saturday evening with a weak upper level \nshortwave near or over eastern Kentucky producing numerous to \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms, with activity quickly winding \ndown through the evening and overnight as the trough passes to the \neast, with heights rising in the wake of this system. This will lead \nto isolated convection Sunday, mainly confined to the higher terrain \ngiven the lack of synoptic scale forcing.\n\nAs we move into the beginning of the work week and into mid-week, we \nwill see upper-level ridging become further established across the \nSoutheast US. This ridging will be quite strong for this time of \nyear, running above the 90th percentile. This will usher in mostly \ndry weather Monday into Tuesday, but with that will come warmer \ntemperatures. The warmest day right now is expected to be Tuesday, \nwith highs topping out in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Then by \nWednesday into Thursday this ridging will start to breakdown and a \ncold front will progress across the Ohio Valley. There has been some \ntiming differences with this boundary within the various model \nsuites that will need to be resolved, but overall expect increasing \nchances of showers and thunderstorms through the mid to late week \nperiod. Then some of the guidance shows this boundary could usher in \nsome much drier and cooler weather by late in the week into next \nweekend.",
